Why the split
-------------

The packs embed grading derived from resources that are NonCommercial or
ShareAlike — CEFRLex (ELELex/FLELex) for the Spanish and French CEFR labels,
hermitdave frequency data (CC BY-SA 4.0) for the derived ordering, KanjiVG
(CC BY-SA 3.0) stroke order and JmdictFurigana (CC BY-SA) furigana for the
Japanese characters stage. An AGPL codebase cannot carry NonCommercial data
without making the licence claim false. Putting the data in a separately
licensed artefact keeps both claims true.

What this means in practice
---------------------------

  * A commercial fork may take the code. It may not take the packs.
  * The licence allow-list is enforced PER ARTEFACT, at ingest (INV-PACK-13):
    NonCommercial data is allowed into packs and forbidden in code.
    NoDerivatives data is forbidden everywhere.
  * Every pack manifest declares the full source table: licence, attribution
    owner and share-alike status for every input.
  * Every sentence requiring attribution is reachable from a rendered surface
    in the app (report sheet, About/credits), not only from a file (INV-PACK-17).

